    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nSuppose that we wish to find the solution to the inequality\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 2x + 6 < 12.]\n\nContent:\nAn inequality like this one can be solved just like an equation, using the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nContent:\nFirst, we apply the addition principle, subtracting [MATH: 6] from both sides:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 2x + 6|< 12; 2x + 6 \u2212 6|< 12 \u2212 6; 2x + 0|< 6; 2x|< 6]\n\nContent:\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by [MATH: 2{:}]\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 2x|< 6; (2x)/(2)|< (6)/(2); (2x)/(2)|< 3; x|< 3]\n\nContent:\nThe solution is [MATH: x < 3] In other words, any value less than [MATH: 3] satisfies the inequality.\n\nContent:\nLet's see another example.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Solving Linear Inequalities\n\nExample Prompt:\nSolve the inequality [MATH: 10x - 4 > 16.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we apply the addition principle, adding [MATH: 4] to both sides:\n\n[MATH: 10x \u2212 4|> 16; 10x \u2212 4 + 4|> 16 + 4; 10x + 0|> 20; 10x|> 20]\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by [MATH: 10{:}]\n\n[MATH: 10x|> 20; (10x)/(10)|> (20)/(10); (10x)/(10)|> 2; x|> 2]\n\nTherefore, the solution is [MATH: x> 2.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nSolve the inequality [MATH: 3x \u2212 5 \u2264 4.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: x \u2264 3]\nb. [MATH: x \u2265 4]\nc. [MATH: x \u2264 4]\nd. [MATH: x \u2265 \u2212 4]\ne. [MATH: x \u2265 3]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nSolve the inequality [MATH: 3c + 1 \u2264 0.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: c \u2264 \u2212 (1)/(3)]\nb. [MATH: c \u2264 (2)/(3)]\nc. [MATH: c \u2265 (1)/(3)]\nd. [MATH: c \u2265 \u2212 (1)/(3)]\ne. [MATH: c \u2265 (2)/(3)]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Solving Linear Inequalities When Flipping is Required\n\nExample Prompt:\nSolve the inequality [MATH: -2x + 3 > -1.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we apply the addition principle, subtracting [MATH: 3] from both sides:\n[MATH: \u2212 2x + 3|> \u2212 1; \u2212 2x + 3 \u2212 3|> \u2212 1 \u2212 3; \u2212 2x + 0|> \u2212 4; \u2212 2x|> \u2212 4]\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by [MATH: -2.]\n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n[MATH: \u2212 2x|> \u2212 4; (\u2212 2x)/(\u2212 2)|<(\u2212 4)/(\u2212 2); (\u2212 2x)/(\u2212 2)|<2; x|<2]\n\nTherefore, the solution is [MATH: x<2.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nSolve the inequality [MATH: \u2212 2x + 3 \u2264 7.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: x \u2265 2]\nb. [MATH: x \u2265 4]\nc. [MATH: x \u2265 \u2212 2]\nd. [MATH: x \u2264 \u2212 2]\ne. [MATH: x \u2264 4]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nSolve the inequality [MATH: \u2212 6x \u2212 5 \u2265 \u2212 8.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: x \u2264 (1)/(2)]\nb. [MATH: x \u2264 (1)/(3)]\nc. [MATH: x \u2264 \u2212 (1)/(2)]\nd. [MATH: x \u2265 (1)/(3)]\ne. [MATH: x \u2265 (1)/(2)]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Solving an Inequality and Adjusting the Final Result\n\nExample Prompt:\nSolve the inequality [MATH: -11 \\le -8y + 5.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we apply the addition principle, subtracting [MATH: 5] from both sides:\n\n[MATH: \u2212 11|\u2264 \u2212 8y + 5; \u2212 11 \u2212 5|\u2264 \u2212 8y + 5 \u2212 5; \u2212 16|\u2264 \u2212 8y + 0; \u2212 16|\u2264 \u2212 8y]\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by [MATH: -8.]\n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n[MATH: \u2212 16|\u2264\u2212 8y; (\u2212 16)/(\u2212 8)|\u2265(\u2212 8y)/(\u2212 8); 2|\u2265(\u2212 8y)/(\u2212 8); 2|\u2265y]\n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember, we also need to flip the inequality.\n\n[MATH: y \\le 2]\n\nTherefore, the solution is [MATH: y \\le 2.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nSolve the inequality [MATH: \u2212 5 < 6w + 7.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: w > \u2212 2]\nb. [MATH: w < 2]\nc. [MATH: w < (1)/(2)]\nd. [MATH: w < \u2212 2]\ne. [MATH: w > 2]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nSolve the inequality [MATH: 6 < \u2212 2y + 7.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: y > (1)/(2)]\nb. [MATH: y < (1)/(2)]\nc. [MATH: y < \u2212 2]\nd. [MATH: y > 2]\ne. [MATH: y < \u2212 (1)/(2)]"
